rounds.response:
Internal memo — concise mapping and safe public lines.

1) Mapping (who’s clearly protected / at significant risk / ambiguous): clearly protected — households in the bottom income deciles if they receive recurring lump‑sum rebates or equivalent increases in means‑tested support, and non‑energy‑intensive small firms with low fuel exposure; significant risk — renters who don’t receive targeted compensation, casual/irregular workers with little savings, thin‑margin local service firms and transport/haulage SMEs, and workers in fossil‑dependent towns; ambiguous — middle‑income, high‑commute households, many SMEs with moderate energy use or partial export exposure, and some EITE manufacturers depending on the final compensation and border measures. [assumption: protections hinge on rebate/transfer and transition funding being passed and reaching intended recipients].

2) Too‑broad phrases to stop using: “most families out here will end up better off overall” and “small businesses will be supported” — both overpromise across the heterogeneous population and collapse if rebates or transition support are watered down. [robust judgment: these claims depend on full, well‑targeted revenue recycling and sized transition measures].

3) Replacement, fact‑check‑resilient lines (short, public‑usable; each bracket flags the contingency): 
- “We will protect low‑income households with a regular, visible rebate designed to more than offset the typical energy bill rise for the poorest families.” [only holds if recurring, well‑targeted rebates for bottom deciles pass]; 
- “We will fund a dedicated regional and worker transition package so fossil‑dependent communities get retraining, local investment and job‑placement support.” [only holds if a sizeable, promptly delivered transition fund is enacted]; 
- “Some local jobs will be lost as the economy shifts, but predictable pricing plus retraining and active local investment will create new opportunities—we will help people move into those jobs.” [robust across scenarios]; 
- “We will offer time‑limited, targeted relief for trade‑exposed, energy‑intensive firms while pursuing border measures to level the international playing field.” [depends on well‑designed EITE relief and credible progress on border carbon measures].

4) Explicit limits and when reassurances fail: these protected outcomes materially weaken if Parliament trims the rebate scheme to one‑offs, underfunds the transition package, or if international competitors refuse comparable pricing—under those conditions renters, casual workers, and thin‑margin firms are far more likely to be worse off and our messaging must switch to acknowledging gaps and promising contingency supports. [key caveat: revenue recycling modality (recurring lump sum vs. tax cuts vs. one‑off credits) is the single biggest determinant of household outcomes].

5) Immediate recommendations: adopt the replacement lines across all spokespeople today, publish a short annex mapping “who is protected” under our current rebate/transition proposal, and prepare rapid local mitigation offers (job clinics, bridge grants) for the most exposed towns so that claims of “we weren’t told” can be met with concrete, deployable responses. [operational: these steps are effective even while legislation is pending; their protective value falls with any fiscal scaling‑back].
